# TAP iOS SDK

When the user wants to build an iOS app with a Tap device, read the `tapkit-getting-started` skill first. Look in `.cursor/skills/tapkit-getting-started/SKILL.md`, then `plugins/tapkit-ios-sdk/skills/tapkit-getting-started/SKILL.md`.

- Order: add the framework and `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, pair the Tap in Settings → Bluetooth, `addDelegate`, `setDefaultTAPInputMode` (and `setDefaultTAPXRState` for TapXR), then `start()`. `start()` alone delivers no taps.
- The SDK connects only to TAP devices already paired in iOS Settings. It does not scan or show a pairing UI.
- One singleton, `TAPKit.sharedKit`. Delegate methods are optional and run on the main queue. `forIdentifiers: nil` targets every connected device.
- Legacy (v1) and V2 devices share the same delegate callbacks. V2-only getters complete with `nil` on legacy hardware.
- `TAPInputMode.text()` types on the system keyboard and does not call `tapped` or `moused`. Use `.controller()` to receive taps in the app.
- V2 air gestures arrive as `TAPXRAirGesture` edges (`tapXRAirGestured`), not as repeating raw model codes. Knob and D-Pad recipes (`tapkit-knob`, `tapkit-dpad`) use those edges.
- Do not invent APIs, Bluetooth UUIDs, or enum values. Test with a real device. There is no simulator.
